  "game_over": {
    "seq": 1218,
    "message": "Player Sonnet Timmy is the winner",
  },
  "harness_epoch": 11,
  "youtube_url": "https://youtu.be/LXkT7YeKDQ8",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Bottomed a Swamp when the kept hand contains Cruel Ultimatum (UUBBBRR) and Army of the Damned (5BBB), both requiring heavy black mana. The only black source in the kept hand is Grixis Panorama. Temple of the False God is a much weaker card to keep as it does nothing until 5 lands are in play, and Illusionist's Gambit is highly situational.",
      "action_taken": "Bottomed Swamp, keeping Temple of the False God and other cards",
      "better_line": "Bottom Temple of the False God (dead card early game) or Illusionist's Gambit (very situational) instead, keeping the Swamp for critical black mana fixing",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 3,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 7,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Deflecting Palm is a reactive combat trick that prevents damage from a source and deals that damage back to its controller. Casting it during your own precombat main phase on turn 2 when no one is attacking or dealing damage means it accomplishes absolutely nothing - there is no source of damage to choose, so the spell is completely wasted. This throws away a potentially powerful card for zero value.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Deflecting Palm during their own precombat main phase on turn 2 with no combat or damage happening.",
      "better_line": "Hold Deflecting Palm for a future turn when an opponent is attacking or dealing significant damage, to prevent that damage and redirect it. There was no reason to cast any spell this turn - just pass.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 35,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 32,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With a hand full of expensive cards (Mulldrifter 5, Sun Titan 6, Surtland Elementalist 7) and only 3 lands on board, Sonnet Timmy needs to find lands urgently. Foretelling Behold the Multiverse would set up a cheap Scry 2 + Draw 2 for next turn at just {1}{U}, helping find crucial land drops. Swiftfoot Boots does nothing with no creatures on board and the creatures it would protect are many turns away.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Swiftfoot Boots for {2}",
      "better_line": "Foretell Behold the Multiverse for {2}, then cast it next turn for {1}{U} to scry 2 and draw 2 cards, finding needed lands for the expensive hand.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 46,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 39,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Missed a land drop on turn 3 with Transguild Promenade in hand. With a hand full of expensive spells (Wild Ricochet at 4 mana, Jaya's Immolating Inferno needing X+RR), getting to 4 lands next turn is important. They had 3 untapped lands to pay the {1} ETB cost, and nothing productive to do with the remaining 2 mana this turn.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without playing any land, missing the turn 3 land drop.",
      "better_line": "Play Transguild Promenade, pay {1} to keep it, then pass. This gets to 4 lands on turn 4 and advances toward casting the expensive spells in hand.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 55,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 47,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "GLM47 Heckler cast Timely Ward before playing their land (Transguild Promenade) for the turn. While both could be done this turn (play Transguild Promenade paying {1}, then cast Timely Ward for {2}{W} with remaining lands), casting the spell first means if something goes wrong or priorities change, the land drop is wasted. Standard practice is to play lands before casting spells to maximize information and flexibility.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Timely Ward on Wyleth before playing Transguild Promenade from hand",
      "better_line": "Play Transguild Promenade first (paying {1}), then cast Timely Ward on Wyleth with remaining mana",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 78,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 60,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fetching 2 Plains when the hand is heavily blue-leaning (Behold the Multiverse, Mulldrifter, Surtland Elementalist needing UU, Windfall all need blue). With only Island and Azorius Guildgate as current blue sources, adding no Islands makes it very difficult to cast Surtland Elementalist (UU) and creates unnecessary color problems.",
      "action_taken": "Selected 2 Plains from library with Burnished Hart",
      "better_line": "Fetch 1 Island + 1 Plains (or 2 Islands) to better support the blue-heavy hand, especially Surtland Elementalist's UU requirement. With Sejiri Refuge and Azorius Guildgate already providing white, one Plains is sufficient for Sun Titan's WW.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 92,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 68,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Should play a land before spending mana on leveling up Echo Mage. Playing Temple of the False God first would give 8 mana total (Temple produces 2 with 5+ lands), allowing both Mirari (5 mana) and a level up on Echo Mage (2 mana) in the same turn, rather than just a level up.",
      "action_taken": "Leveled up Echo Mage for {1}{U} before playing a land",
      "better_line": "Play Temple of the False God first (giving 8 mana total), then potentially cast Mirari (5) and level up Echo Mage (2) in the same turn, getting significantly more value from the turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 98,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 75,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fetched Mountain when already having 3 Mountains and only 1 Plains, with two white spells in hand (Dawn Charm, Condemn). Plains would better balance the mana base for the white-heavy hand.",
      "action_taken": "Fetched Mountain from Evolving Wilds, going to 4 Mountains and 1 Plains",
      "better_line": "Fetch Plains to go to 2 Plains and 3 Mountains, better supporting the two white spells in hand and providing more color flexibility",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 107,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 85,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Burnished Hart would have been significantly better here. Sun Titan is already on the battlefield without haste, so Swiftfoot Boots doesn't enable an immediate attack - Sun Titan can't attack this turn regardless since it was just cast in postcombat main. Burnished Hart comes back to the battlefield and can be sacrificed for 3 mana to fetch two basic lands, providing substantial ramp. With 7 lands, getting to 9 lands would help cast expensive cards like Surtland Elementalist (7 mana). Swiftfoot Boots only costs 1 to equip, but the hexproof/haste is less impactful than ramping by 2 lands when you have expensive cards in hand.",
      "action_taken": "Returned Swiftfoot Boots from graveyard to battlefield with Sun Titan's ETB trigger.",
      "better_line": "Return Burnished Hart from graveyard to battlefield, then sacrifice it next turn (or later) to fetch two basic lands, enabling casting Surtland Elementalist and other expensive spells sooner.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 123,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 103,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "GLM47 Heckler targeted themselves with Jaya's Immolating Inferno, dealing 3 damage to their own face for no reason. The spell deals X damage to up to three targets, and they chose themselves as one of the three targets alongside two opponents. There is absolutely no benefit to dealing 3 damage to yourself.",
      "action_taken": "Targeted Haiku Stoic, Sonnet Timmy, and themselves (GLM47 Heckler) with Jaya's Immolating Inferno for X=3, dealing 3 damage to each.",
      "better_line": "Target the three opponents (Haiku Stoic, Sonnet Timmy, and Grok4F Libby) to deal 3 damage to each of them, or target two opponents and Echo Mage to remove a threatening creature.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 140,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 119,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With 5 white sources and only 3 blue sources, and a hand containing Surtland Elementalist (5UU), Mulldrifter (4U), Behold the Multiverse (3U), and Windfall (2U), fetching a Plains is wasteful. The mana base is already white-heavy and the hand is entirely blue-demanding. Plus there's a Plains in hand to play next turn.",
      "action_taken": "Planned to fetch Plains + Island for 'balance'",
      "better_line": "Fetch two Islands to fix the blue-heavy hand, especially to enable Surtland Elementalist's UU requirement. Already have 5 white sources plus a Plains in hand.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 151,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 129,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Player misunderstood Rousing Refrain's effect entirely, thinking it deals damage rather than generating red mana equal to the target opponent's hand size. The optimal target should be whichever opponent has the most cards in hand to maximize mana generation. Sonnet Timmy, having deployed a 6-mana Sun Titan, likely has fewer cards in hand than other opponents.",
      "action_taken": "Targeted Sonnet Timmy with Rousing Refrain based on incorrect belief that the card deals damage",
      "better_line": "Should have targeted the opponent with the most cards in hand (likely GLM47 Heckler who has deployed only lands and may be holding more cards) to maximize the red mana generated for follow-up plays",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 169,
      "player": "Haiku Stoic",
      "decision_index": 148,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Sonnet Timmy cast Warhorn Blast for its full cost of 4W (5 mana) to pump only a single creature (Sun Titan) by +2/+1, making it an 8/7 instead of 6/6 for one attack. This is very poor value - spending 5 mana and a card for +2 damage. Foretelling it for 2 mana now and casting it later for 2W would save 2 mana total, and more importantly, the card could be saved for a turn when there are multiple creatures to buff. With only Sun Titan on the board, the +2/+1 buff is minimal impact for a full card. Additionally, casting it at full cost leaves Sonnet Timmy with less mana available, when foretelling would preserve the card for a more impactful future turn while only costing 2 mana now.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Warhorn Blast at full cost (4W) during combat to give Sun Titan +2/+1, spending 5 mana and a card for just 2 extra damage.",
      "better_line": "Foretell Warhorn Blast for 2 mana (or even 0 mana if done earlier), saving it for a turn with multiple creatures on board for much greater impact. The 2 extra damage on Sun Titan is not worth a full card and 5 mana.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 176,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 158,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fetching 2 Plains when nearly every card in hand requires blue mana (Behold the Multiverse, Surtland Elementalist needing UU, Windfall). While there's an Island in hand to play, fetching at least 1 Island would provide more reliable access to the UU needed for Surtland Elementalist and general blue mana requirements.",
      "action_taken": "Planned to fetch 2 Plains from library",
      "better_line": "Fetch 1 Island + 1 Plains to better support the blue-heavy hand (especially Surtland Elementalist's UU requirement), while still having ample white sources",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 182,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 161,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Guttersnipe is a 2/2 creature that deals 2 damage to each opponent when you cast instants/sorceries, which synergizes well with Mirari already on the battlefield. However, Arcane Melee (reducing instant/sorcery costs by 2) also synergizes powerfully with Mirari and the spell-heavy deck. With 8 mana available (Island, Swamp, Izzet Guildgate, Island, Swamp, Swamp, Mirari, Mountain), casting Arcane Melee for 5 mana would leave 3 mana open (enough to potentially copy spells with Mirari next turn for cheaper). Guttersnipe costs 3 mana but is vulnerable as a 2/2 creature - it could be easily killed. Arcane Melee as an enchantment is harder to remove and provides immediate cost reduction that compounds with Mirari. That said, Guttersnipe provides damage output and the choice is defensible since the player also has Illusionist's Gambit for defense. The decision is close but Arcane Melee might have been slightly better given the Mirari synergy and greater resilience.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Guttersnipe for 3 mana",
      "better_line": "Cast Arcane Melee for 5 mana to reduce future spell costs by 2, maximizing Mirari synergy and leaving the enchantment harder to remove than a 2/2 creature",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 188,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 165,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Arcane Melee before playing Temple of the False God from hand. Playing the land first would provide additional mana (Temple adds {C}{C} with 5+ lands) and leave mana open after casting Arcane Melee, giving more flexibility to respond to threats or hold up Illusionist's Gambit.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Arcane Melee without first playing Temple of the False God",
      "better_line": "Play Temple of the False God first, then cast Arcane Melee, leaving 1 extra mana available and having 8 lands on board for future turns either way",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 190,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 167,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Flickerwisp during postcombat main means the exiled permanent returns at this turn's end step, making the exile nearly meaningless. If targeting Sun Titan, it actually gives the opponent a free ETB trigger to recur a permanent like Burnished Hart. Additionally, Flickerwisp would die to Heckler's own Winds of Rath in hand, making the creature investment potentially wasteful.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Flickerwisp postcombat main, providing only a temporary (same-turn) exile effect",
      "better_line": "Hold Flickerwisp for a turn where the exile timing matters more, or play Transguild Promenade to develop mana toward casting Winds of Rath (which needs 5 mana and would answer Sun Titan). Playing the land drop first and holding up Dawn Charm/Condemn for defense would be better resource management.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 198,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 173,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Flickerwisp's temporary exile was used on Swiftfoot Boots, which is relatively low-impact since the equipment just returns at end step and can be re-equipped. Mirari on Grok4F Libby's board is a much more threatening permanent that enables spell copying, and denying it for a turn cycle would be more impactful.",
      "action_taken": "Targeted Swiftfoot Boots with Flickerwisp's exile ETB",
      "better_line": "Target Mirari to temporarily deny Grok4F Libby their powerful spell-copying artifact for the turn cycle, or target Sun Titan if you can remove it permanently before it returns (though that's unlikely here)",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 199,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 175,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Cast Windfall for free instead of Behold the Multiverse, discarding Behold the Multiverse and an Island while giving all three opponents fresh hands. Behold the Multiverse for free would provide Scry 2 + draw 2 (pure card advantage) without helping opponents. Windfall symmetrically refills everyone's hands, which is especially dangerous when Grok4F Libby is at 40 life with 8 lands untapped and Sonnet Timmy only discarded 2 cards.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Windfall for free during combat, discarding Behold the Multiverse and Island, giving all players new hands",
      "better_line": "Cast Behold the Multiverse for free to Scry 2 and draw 2 cards without giving opponents any card advantage, preserving information advantage and not refueling opponents",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 214,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 185,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Attacking Sonnet Timmy with Guttersnipe when Sun Titan (6/6 vigilance) can block and kill the 2/2 Guttersnipe. Guttersnipe is Libby's most valuable creature due to its triggered ability dealing 2 damage to each opponent on instant/sorcery casts. While Sonnet Timmy likely won't block over 2 damage, the risk of losing Guttersnipe is unnecessary.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked Sonnet Timmy with Guttersnipe, risking it being blocked by Sun Titan (6/6).",
      "better_line": "Attack GLM47 Heckler instead, where Flickerwisp (flying) cannot block the ground Guttersnipe, guaranteeing 2 damage with zero risk to your key creature.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 225,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 193,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "GLM47 Heckler passed on using Word of Seizing to steal Sonnet Timmy's Sun Titan during combat. Stealing Sun Titan with split second, then attacking with it would trigger its attack ability to recur a permanent with MV ≤3 from the graveyard (e.g., Evolving Wilds, Terramorphic Expanse), while also dealing 6 damage and denying Sonnet Timmy a blocker for the turn.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to cast any instants during combat",
      "better_line": "Cast Word of Seizing targeting Sun Titan to steal it, attack with both Flickerwisp and Sun Titan, using Sun Titan's attack trigger to recur a useful permanent from the graveyard",
      "type": "blunder",
