      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 208,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "GLM47 Heckler is enchanting Sram, Senior Edificer with Spirit Mantle, but Sram is their own creature that appears to be a 2/2 legendary. While Sram draws a card when an Aura is cast (which triggers here), putting Spirit Mantle on Sram gives it +1/+1 and protection from creatures, making it a 3/3 unblockable attacker. However, Flickerwisp (3/1 flyer) is already a better attacker, and Sun Titan (6/6 with vigilance) belonging to Sonnet Timmy is a much bigger threat on the board. The Spirit Mantle on Flickerwisp would make a 4/2 unblockable flyer, which deals more damage per turn. On the other hand, Sram with Spirit Mantle creates a Voltron engine where future Auras and Equipment draw cards and the creature can't be blocked by creatures. This is a reasonable strategic choice for building toward a Voltron plan, especially since Sram's draw trigger already fired. The choice between Flickerwisp and Sram is debatable - Flickerwisp might get flickered or die more easily as a 3/1, while Sram as a Voltron target has long-term value with its card draw ability.",
      "action_taken": "Enchanted Sram, Senior Edificer with Spirit Mantle",
      "better_line": "Enchanting Flickerwisp with Spirit Mantle would create a 4/2 unblockable flyer for more immediate damage output, though Sram as a Voltron target has reasonable long-term synergy.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 239,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 215,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Spectral Deluge with only 1 Island on the battlefield means X=1, so it only bounces creatures with toughness 1 or less. This wastes a powerful late-game sweeper for minimal impact (only bouncing Flickerwisp). With 2 Islands still in hand, waiting to play more Islands would have made this card significantly more powerful.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Spectral Deluge with X=1, bouncing only toughness-1-or-less creatures",
      "better_line": "Either cast Cleansing Nova (artifacts/enchantments mode to remove Arcane Melee), or hold Spectral Deluge until more Islands are in play for a much more impactful bounce effect. Playing Islands from hand first would have increased X.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 244,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 222,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Arcane Melee is a symmetric cost reduction that helps all players equally. Spirit Mantle on Sun Titan makes it a 7/7 with protection from creatures (unblockable in combat), which is a much more pressing threat. Propaganda also directly prevents GLM47 Heckler from attacking Grok4F Libby.",
      "action_taken": "Selected Arcane Melee as a Return to Dust target",
      "better_line": "Should have prioritized Spirit Mantle (making Sun Titan unblockable) or Propaganda (preventing attacks on Libby) as the higher-value exile target",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 246,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 231,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Spirit Mantle is likely enchanting Sun Titan, making it a 7/7 with protection from creatures (unblockable, can't be blocked). This is a far more dangerous combination than Swiftfoot Boots, which only provides hexproof and haste to its equipped creature. An unblockable Sun Titan that recurs permanents every attack is one of the most threatening board states possible.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to exile Swiftfoot Boots as the second Return to Dust target",
      "better_line": "Exile Spirit Mantle to remove protection from creatures from Sun Titan, making it blockable and vulnerable to creature-based interaction",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 246,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 231,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Spiteful Visions deals 1 damage to each player whenever they draw a card, and gives each player an extra draw per turn. In a multiplayer game where one opponent (Haiku Stoic) is already eliminated, Grok4F Libby is at 40 life (the highest), GLM47 Heckler is at 23, and Sonnet Timmy is at 35. While Spiteful Visions does asymmetrically hurt the lower-life players more, it also gives all opponents extra cards and hurts Libby too. Sonnet Timmy has Sun Titan on board and will benefit from extra draws to find more threats. Charmbreaker Devils (5R) would be castable with 8 lands and would start recurring instants/sorceries from the graveyard while being a 4/4 threat. Decree of Pain (6BB) is not yet castable. The Spiteful Visions play is defensible as it pressures the lower-life Heckler, but giving Sonnet Timmy (who has Sun Titan and a developed board) extra cards is risky.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Spiteful Visions (4 mana) to deal damage on draws and grant extra draws to all players.",
      "better_line": "Cast Charmbreaker Devils (6 mana, available with 8 lands including Izzet Boilerworks producing 2) to start recurring instants/sorceries from graveyard and present a threat, or cast Temple Bell + Swiftfoot Boots for more controlled card advantage.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 255,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 234,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Sonnet Timmy cast Cloudblazer before playing Island from hand and before attacking with Sun Titan. Playing Island first would give 8 lands, meaning after casting Cloudblazer (5 mana), they'd have 3 mana remaining - enough for Ghostly Prison. Additionally, attacking with Sun Titan first would trigger its attack ability to return a permanent with MV 3 or less from graveyard (e.g., Burnished Hart for ramp) before committing mana to Cloudblazer.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Cloudblazer without playing Island first and before combat",
      "better_line": "Play Island, go to combat, attack with Sun Titan (returning Burnished Hart or another valuable permanent from graveyard), then cast Cloudblazer in second main phase with more mana available for additional plays",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 275,
