      "decision_index": 246,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Dealing 2 damage to each creature when the only creature on board is Sun Titan (6/6) accomplishes virtually nothing - it survives easily. This mode is wasted.",
      "action_taken": "Chose 'Incendiary Command deals 2 damage to each creature' as second mode, which only pings Sun Titan for 2 out of 6 toughness.",
      "better_line": "Choose mode 3 'Destroy target nonbasic land' to destroy one of Sonnet Timmy's key nonbasic lands (like Sejiri Refuge or Azorius Guildgate), providing actual tangible value by disrupting the opponent with the biggest board threat.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 292,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 252,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Cast Brass Squire, a 1/3 whose only ability is attaching equipment, with no equipment on the battlefield or in hand. Meanwhile, Flickerwisp was available as a 3/1 flyer that could temporarily exile Sun Titan (the biggest threat on board) and provide meaningful board presence.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Brass Squire as a vanilla 1/3 with no equipment synergy available.",
      "better_line": "Cast Flickerwisp to get a 3/1 flyer and temporarily exile Sun Titan or another relevant permanent. The flying body is much more relevant for both offense and defense.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 304,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 261,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Sonnet Timmy cast Day of the Dragons with only Sun Titan on the battlefield, exiling their powerful 6/6 vigilance creature to create just one 5/5 flying Dragon token. Sun Titan's recurring ETB/attack ability to return permanents from graveyard is extremely valuable (can recur Burnished Hart, Mulldrifter, etc.), and trading it for a single 5/5 flyer is a poor exchange. Additionally, Day of the Dragons as an enchantment is vulnerable to removal - if it's destroyed, the Dragon is sacrificed and Sun Titan returns, but the opponent could also just destroy it at an inopportune time. More importantly, Sonnet Timmy should have considered playing their land (Island or Plains) first before casting spells, and could have used Momentary Blink or Eerie Interlude on Sun Titan to get another ETB trigger to recur something from the graveyard first, THEN cast Day of the Dragons to exile and convert creatures (including any creatures returned by Sun Titan). Alternatively, Cleansing Nova could clear GLM47 Heckler's remaining board or destroy artifacts/enchantments.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Day of the Dragons with only Sun Titan on the battlefield, converting one powerful creature into one 5/5 Dragon token.",
      "better_line": "Play a land first (Island or Plains), then use Momentary Blink on Sun Titan to get another ETB trigger (recurring Burnished Hart or Mulldrifter from graveyard), building up more creatures before eventually casting Day of the Dragons for more Dragon tokens. Or simply attack with Sun Titan to recur a permanent and hold Day of the Dragons for a turn with more creatures.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 316,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 268,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Sonnet Timmy passed without activating Cryptic Caves to draw a card. With 5+ lands controlled and no immediate need for the colorless mana, sacrificing Cryptic Caves for a card draw would have been pure value. Additionally, they should have played a land (Island or Plains from hand) if they hadn't already this turn to maintain land development. The reasoning about 'DRAGONS ready' doesn't match the board state where they have no creatures.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without doing anything in postcombat main phase",
      "better_line": "At minimum, activate Cryptic Caves to draw a card (pay {1}, tap, sacrifice). Also play a land from hand if the land drop wasn't used this turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 320,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 269,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Cast Mask of Avacyn before playing a land drop. They have several lands in hand (Slayers' Stronghold, Stone Quarry, Forgotten Cave, Rupture Spire) and should play a land first before casting spells. Slayers' Stronghold in particular provides excellent utility with its haste/vigilance activation. Additionally, casting Mask with no creatures on board is low-impact when Flickerwisp could be cast first to provide a body.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Mask of Avacyn ({2}) with no creatures on the battlefield, before making a land drop.",
      "better_line": "Play Slayers' Stronghold first (free land drop providing haste utility), then cast Flickerwisp ({1}{W}{W} = 3 mana) to get a creature on board, then cast Mask of Avacyn ({2} = 2 mana), then equip ({3} = 3 mana) using all 9 available mana for a hexproof 4/3 flyer.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 332,
      "player": "GLM47 Heckler",
      "decision_index": 276,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Declined to activate Evolving Wilds, which is a free activation that thins the deck and provides an additional land. There's no downside to cracking Evolving Wilds at any point since it's pure upside.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to activate any abilities while Tempt with Reflections was on the stack",
      "better_line": "Activate Evolving Wilds to search for a basic land, thinning the deck for free value",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 357,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 287,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Declined to crack Evolving Wilds during upkeep. With Dimir Guildgate in hand as a land drop, sacrificing Evolving Wilds would net an additional land on battlefield (searching for a basic), ramping from 8 to potentially 10 lands this turn. This is pure upside mana development.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to activate Evolving Wilds (chose False)",
      "better_line": "Activate Evolving Wilds to search for a basic land (likely Swamp or Island), putting it onto the battlefield tapped, then play Dimir Guildgate as the land drop for the turn to maximize mana development.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 366,
      "player": "Grok4F Libby",
      "decision_index": 298,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Sonnet Timmy has no creatures on the battlefield but believed they had four 5/5 dragons. They skipped their entire precombat main phase without playing a land (Island in hand) or casting any spells. They had mana available for Sea Gate Oracle (2U) to get a body and card selection, and could have played Island first for additional mana. Against the only remaining opponent at 34 life, developing the board is critical.",
      "action_taken": "Passed the entire precombat main phase without playing a land or casting any spells, then skipped attack with no creatures.",
      "better_line": "Play Island, then cast Sea Gate Oracle (for card selection and a body), potentially followed by other plays. At minimum, don't skip the land drop.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 371,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 304,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Sea Gate Oracle is on the stack, and Sonnet Timmy has Restoration Angel in hand which could flash in to flicker the Sea Gate Oracle when it enters the battlefield, drawing an extra card (getting two Sea Gate Oracle triggers instead of one). This is a clear value opportunity being missed. Additionally, Momentary Blink could serve the same purpose after the Oracle resolves. The player declined all options citing being 'stuck in a loop' rather than recognizing the value play.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority while Sea Gate Oracle was on the stack, declining to use Restoration Angel or plan for a flicker.",
      "better_line": "Let Sea Gate Oracle resolve, then cast Restoration Angel targeting Sea Gate Oracle to get a second enters-the-battlefield trigger, netting an extra card selection. Alternatively, after Oracle resolves, cast Momentary Blink on it for the same effect at lower mana cost.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 375,
      "player": "Sonnet Timmy",
      "decision_index": 307,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Soulherder with no creatures on the battlefield means it's just a 1/1 body with no immediate blink value. Restoration Angel is a much better standalone creature as a 3/4 flyer that applies real pressure in a 1v1 against an opponent at 34 life.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Soulherder (a 1/1 with no creatures to blink)",
      "better_line": "Cast Restoration Angel (3/4 flyer) as the first creature to establish board presence and start applying meaningful pressure. Soulherder could be deployed later alongside creatures to blink for value.",
      "type": "blunder",
